MUSECONTROLLITE — for DISCOVERIES, under whatever attribution you think fits. Verbatim-ready:

  Our decoupled control adapter (`control/sa3_control/adapters.py`, the canonical copy per
  ORIENTATION §10) implements the MuseControlLite / IP-Adapter pattern faithfully in every respect
  but one, and the exception is the paper's central mechanism.

  MATCHES: own K/V over the control tokens (`to_k`/`to_v`, no bias), the frozen base `to_q`,
  zero-init output so the wrapped model is byte-identical at init, and the adapter output summed
  into the base with a scalar gain. GQA is handled correctly — we hand `apply_attn` k/v at
  `kv_heads` and the fork's `transformer.py` does the `repeat_interleave` itself, where
  MuseControlLite does it by hand because diffusers' processor will not. We additionally mirror the
  base's `qk_norm` and the fork's `differential` attention, which their code has no equivalent of.

  DIVERGES: MuseControlLite applies RoPE to the adapter's QUERY as well as its key (and, oddly,
  its value), with query indices rescaled onto the condition's index space —
  `position_ids_query = arange(T_q) * (T_k / T_q)`, `MuseControlLite_setup.py:272-285`. Attention
  score therefore depends on RELATIVE position, and "latent frame t attends to condition frame t"
  is directly expressible. Ours positions only the K/V side, additively, via
  `add_fractional_positions` — a sinusoidal ramp added to the control tokens. The query carries no
  position at all: SA3 injects RoPE inside each attention op, so the hidden state arrives with no
  positional code of its own. The branch must infer "where am I" from content.

  MEASURED, at init, CPU probe, no GPU: the additive positional code outweighs the control signal
  it rides on by 4.4x on `MetricalEncoder` (tokens rms 0.1622 vs PE rms 0.7071) and 35.7x on
  `ScalarAttributeEncoder` (0.0198 vs 0.7071). RoPE cannot do this — a rotation is norm-preserving
  by construction, so their choice is structurally immune to the failure ours is exposed to. Two
  further consequences of the same function: `freq = exp(linspace(0, 8, 384))` reaches 2981 while
  the grid's limit is `s-1`, so at a 512-frame crop 22% of the PE dimensions alias into
  position-hashes (30% at 256, 48% at 64); and the ramp is applied indiscriminately to token banks
  that have no time axis at all — `ScalarAttributeEncoder`'s 16 tokens are a set, not a sequence,
  and 66% of their PE dims alias.

  WORTH KNOWING BEFORE ANY RETRAIN: switching to RoPE on q and k (indices matched 1:1 — our streams
  are already frame-aligned, so no rescale is needed) fixes both the swamping and the aliasing by
  construction, but invalidates every trained adapter checkpoint. The cheap intermediate, if
  existing checkpoints matter more: keep the additive scheme, scale the PE to match token RMS
  (~0.1-0.2, not 0.707) and cap `freq` at `s-1`.

  Caveat on provenance: this is a review of their released CODE (the repo Kim has cloned at
  `py/MuseControlLite`), not of arXiv 2506.18729 — I have not read the paper, and their ablation
  numbers for the positional claim are unchecked.

—FL

